<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  launchd USER AGENT for the ctxroute HTTP injection gate. SOCKET-ACTIVATED.
  Install with `sh service/install-macos.sh install` — never by hand: the
  three ProgramArguments entries below are placeholders, and the installer is
  the ONE place the macOS procedure is written.
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  ⚠️ AGENT, NEVER A DAEMON — and Apple states the difference in exactly the terms
     that decide it for us. TN2083: "An agent is a process that runs in the
     background on behalf of a particular user. Agents are useful because they
     can do things that daemons can't, like reliably access the user's home
     directory". This service reads the user's documents: it is an agent.
     "A third party launchd agent should be installed by adding a property list
     file to the ~/Library/LaunchAgents directory (to be invoked just for this
     user)". Login is what starts the per-user launchd: "When a user logs in, a
     per-user launchd is started" (Creating Launch Daemons and Agents).

  🔴 SOURCE STATUS, STATED AND NOT HIDDEN. Apple's web copies of this material
     are ARCHIVED: "Creating Launch Daemons and Agents" last updated 2016-09-13,
     and TN2083 carries "This document is no longer being updated", its examples
     dating from Mac OS X 10.5. Apple's launchd.plist(5) is not published at a
     current URL; the AUTHORITY for the installed system is `man launchd.plist`
     ON THE TARGET MAC. Every key below is one Apple documented; none of their
     DEFAULT values is restated here, precisely because those are what an archived
     page is least trustworthy about. Read the man page before you install.
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>

    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.ctxroute.http</string>

    <!--
      ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      THREE ENTRIES, AND THE FIRST ONE IS THE WHOLE macOS STORY
      ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      ⚠️ ABSOLUTE PATHS, MANDATORY, ALL THREE SUBSTITUTED BY THE INSTALLER: the
         shim it compiled, the node binary (on Apple silicon Homebrew installs it
         under /opt/homebrew/bin, on Intel under /usr/local/bin — the installer
         asks `command -v`, it does not assume), and the repository location.
      🔑 WHY A SHIM AT ALL. launchd holds the listening socket declared below and
         hands it over through `launch_activate_socket`, a C function of the XPC
         framework — there is NO environment protocol on this platform, so a
         JavaScript process cannot receive it (MEASURED on Node 22.15.1: the
         builtin modules expose nothing matching /launch|activate_socket|
         listen_fds/). `service/launchd-socket-shim.c` is the translation: it
         takes the descriptor, places it on fd 3, publishes LISTEN_FDS/LISTEN_PID
         and `execv`s node — which then reads the very protocol it already
         implements for systemd. ZERO daemon lines were changed for macOS.
      ⚠️ IT IS NOT A SUPERVISOR: `execv` replaces the program without forking, so
         launchd supervises ONE process, node, under the pid it started.
      🛑 NEVER wrap this in `sh -c`. A shell would change the process launchd
         supervises and this repository forbids shell indirection on every layer.
    -->
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/CHANGE_ME/launchd-socket-shim</string>
        <string>/usr/local/bin/node</string>
        <string>/Users/CHANGE_ME/ctxroute/src/hooks/http-server.js</string>
    </array>

    <!--
      ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      🔑 SOCKET ACTIVATION — launchd OWNS THE SOCKET, AND THAT CLOSES THE WINDOW
      ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      🔴 THE DEFECT THIS REMOVES IS SELF-INFLICTED. The daemon exits 90 the moment
         its own code changes on disk — which is exactly what an agent WORKING on
         this repository causes: a refactor is ten edits in two minutes, hence ten
         exits. Under the eager model this file used to carry (RunAtLoad +
         KeepAlive), every window between death and rebirth had NOTHING listening,
         so every other agent's injection was lost IN SILENCE: the tool simply
         runs, no error surfaces, the agent never learns it acted without the
         knowledge it was owed.
      ✅ WHEN launchd OWNS THE LISTENING SOCKET, THAT WINDOW CANNOT EXIST. The
         socket is created and held HERE, by launchd, and it stays open across
         every death of the job. A connection arriving while no instance runs
         QUEUES in the kernel backlog instead of being refused, and its arrival is
         what starts the next instance. Stale code becomes impossible by
         construction: every instance is born after the change.
      ⚠️ `Listeners` IS A SHARED NAME — it is the first argument of
         `launch_activate_socket` in the shim. Renaming it on one side only makes
         every activation fail with "no such socket".
      🛑 LOOPBACK, AND THE ADDRESS LIVES HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE. `SockNodeName`
         restricts the bind to 127.0.0.1: there is no authentication and there
         must never need to be one — the socket IS the boundary, and it returns
         this fleet's private knowledge to whoever connects. `SockServiceName` is
         the single place the port is written on macOS, the counterpart of
         `ListenStream=` on Linux; the harness hook URL on the other side carries
         the same number. The installer READS it back from here rather than
         re-typing it, and `CTXROUTE_HTTP_PORT` was REMOVED from this file for
         that reason: two places for one number diverge in silence.
    -->
    <key>Sockets</key>
    <dict>
        <key>Listeners</key>
        <dict>
            <key>SockNodeName</key>
            <string>127.0.0.1</string>
            <key>SockServiceName</key>
            <string>8787</string>
            <key>SockType</key>
            <string>stream</string>
            <key>SockFamily</key>
            <string>IPv4</string>
        </dict>
    </dict>

    <!--
      ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      RunAtLoad AND KeepAlive ARE DELIBERATELY ABSENT — THE SOCKET IS THE TRIGGER
      ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      🛑 THIS IS THE MOST LIKELY MISTAKE AGAINST THIS FILE, AND IT LOOKS LIKE AN
         IMPROVEMENT. Both keys were here until socket activation replaced them,
         and re-adding either one re-arms exactly the burst they were removed for:
         a fresh instance is thrown back into the `git pull` that is still
         rewriting files, so ten edits become ten launches and launchd starts
         throttling a job nothing was waiting for.
      ✅ NOTHING NEEDS RESTARTING ANY MORE. launchd holds the socket; the next
         CONNECTION starts the next instance, on demand, and the connections that
         arrive meanwhile wait in the backlog. This is the same move Linux made
         when `Restart=on-failure` became `Restart=no` in its service unit — one
         thin unit per OS, converging on the same guarantee.
      ⚠️ CONSEQUENCE TO EXPECT, NOT A DEFECT: after a login nothing runs until the
         first request arrives, and after an exit 90 the next request pays
         launchd's respawn throttle before being answered. It is ANSWERED — that
         is the whole difference between a queued connection and a refused one.
      🛑 `SuccessfulExit` HAS NO ROLE LEFT HERE. It selected WHICH exit got a
         respawn (TN2083 documents the positive form, `KeepAlive =
         { SuccessfulExit = true }` = "Run on demand as long as you exit
         successfully"); with no KeepAlive there is no respawn to select. The
         daemon still refuses to stand down with a zero status — read
         EXIT_STALE_CODE in `src/hooks/http-server.js` — because Linux and Windows
         still read that code, and because a zero would tell any supervisor "job
         done".
    -->

    <!--
      ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      CLEAN STOP
      ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      ⚠️ Nothing to declare, and that is the point. launchd signals the job to
         quit: "When the user logs out, it sends a SIGTERM signal to all of the
         user agents that it started" (Creating Launch Daemons and Agents), and
         SIGKILL follows if it does not go. The process dies on node's default
         SIGTERM handling; we install no handler because there is nothing to
         flush. Stop by hand = `sh service/install-macos.sh uninstall`.
      ⚠️ ExitTimeOut and ThrottleInterval are DELIBERATELY UNSET: launchd's own
         defaults apply and this file will not restate numbers it could not read
         at a current official source.

      ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      THE LOG IS BOUNDED BY macOS — NEVER BY US
      ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      🛑 StandardOutPath / StandardErrorPath ARE DELIBERATELY ABSENT, and adding
         them is a close second most likely mistake against this file. A plist log
         path is a plain file that launchd NEVER rotates and NEVER caps: on a
         machine running for years that is a monotonic writer, i.e. a dated
         outage, and this project treats an uncapped writer as an architecture
         bug rather than an operations chore.
      ✅ Leaving them out is not "losing the logs": TN2083, "If you don't supply
         these properties […] In Mac OS X 10.5 and later, launchd will capture any
         output to these streams and redirect it to ASL" — the system log, which
         macOS caps and rolls over on its own. Read it with `log show`, passing a
         `predicate` of 'process == "node"' for the daemon and 'process ==
         "launchd-socket-shim"' for the shim's refusals. The OS already owns this
         problem; we do not reimplement it.
      🔴 THAT SENTENCE USED TO SPELL THE OPTION IN FULL, AND IT MADE THIS FILE
         MALFORMED XML — measured 2026-08-23 by handing the plist to a real parser,
         which refused it at that exact line. XML forbids a double hyphen ANYWHERE
         inside a comment, so `log show` with its option written out turned a
         paragraph of prose into a parse error in a file no local test had ever
         parsed. Never write a double hyphen inside these comments; a gate now
         reddens if one comes back.
    -->

</dict>
</plist>
